Options -Indexes

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# This file is load-bearing on a shared host that does not grant
# AllowOverride All, and the two halves below fail very differently without it.
#
# `Options -Indexes` (above) needs the Options directive. The rewrite block needs
# mod_rewrite. A host that honours this file with AllowOverride FileInfo — a very
# common default — rejects `Options` outright and returns 500 for every URL under
# this directory, which takes the site's content with it. A host without
# mod_rewrite is worse in a quieter way: the rewrite block never runs, so the
# three public payloads stop being served and the site renders empty, with no
# error in any log to explain it. The <IfModule> guard below is what keeps the
# explicit denies alive in that case; it was missing, and the comment that
# promised "belt and braces" protection below described a block that could never
# execute.
#
# Nothing in this directory is a public resource except the three
# derived payloads at the bottom. Everything else is either the admin's
# private record or runtime state.
#
# Anything that is "deny by default" is enforced below with a rewrite
# rule rather than <FilesMatch>, because FilesMatch only ever sees the
# *filename* — it cannot match a path, so <FilesMatch "^cache/"> silently
# does nothing for files sitting inside a subdirectory of the same name.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------

# Every request that reaches this directory must name one of the allowed
# files below. Anything else — content.json, the lock, the error log, the
# translation cache, future additions — is denied without having to be
# remembered here.
#
# The allowed public surface is:
#   uploads/            the image library the blog, OG tags and projects
#                       reference by URL
#   content-site.json   the homepage payload
#   content-blog.json   the blog listing payload
#   content-posts.json  the full article bodies
#
# uploads/ is allowed here, in the *parent* context, rather than in
# data/uploads/.htaccess on purpose. A per-directory rewrite chain is merged
# child-first, so a "stop processing" rule in the child does win — but the
# parent is the only context that can see the whole path, and matching
# `^uploads/` here is what keeps the catch-all below from ever applying to
# the images. The child's .htaccess keeps the responsibility that only makes
# sense down there: never execute anything, never list the directory.
#
# No RewriteBase on purpose: the site may live in a subdirectory, and these
# rules only match (they never substitute a path), so a per-directory context
# is all that is needed and nothing has to be configured per deployment.
<IfModule mod_rewrite.c>
  RewriteEngine On
  RewriteCond %{REQUEST_FILENAME} !-f
  RewriteRule ^ - [R=404,L]
  RewriteRule ^uploads/ - [L]
  RewriteRule ^(content-site|content-blog|content-posts|card|views)\.json$ - [L]
  RewriteRule ^ - [F,L]
</IfModule>

# Belt and braces for the case where AllowOverride does not permit
# mod_rewrite: keep the explicit denies for the paths already known.
<FilesMatch "^content\.json$">
  Require all denied
</FilesMatch>
<FilesMatch "^\.">
  Require all denied
</FilesMatch>
<FilesMatch "\.(log|txt|json\.bak)$">
  Require all denied
</FilesMatch>
